---
// The privacy policy. Written from what the software actually does, and checked against the
// code: what a self-hosted instance sends a network (counts, signed receipts, and for customers a
// first-contact email and the passes their assistants carry), only for networks the owner turns
// on; what our network keeps and for how long; what a customer can do about it; what the hosted
// service would hold; what the site itself collects; and what Search (its MCP server at /mcp, A2A,
// the REST API) and the checker keep: the service's log keeps no search, a check keeps the site and never who
// asked, register/update keep the listing and keyed hashes of their proof, and the request log
// holds no IP address and no query string (the front server's error log can hold both, and the
// headers). Those logs have no time limit yet: the logs bullet names one only once it is set. Requests
// the MCP server carries to a business's inbox (ADR-025) pass through and are not written down; one
// record of each is kept (when, which business, which kind, the outcome), 400 days. The phone app
// (ADR-028): what the inbox, the push relay (docs/protocol/push-relay.md) and Google see, the update
// check against /phone/latest.json, and what stays on the phone.
// It is a draft for a lawyer to review before the hosted service opens, and it says so.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { DOOR_SHARE_SHOWN, doorHistoryPublic } from "../lib/door-history";
import { CARRY_PROMISE, HOSTED_KEEPS } from "../lib/gateway";
import { HOSTED_PILOT_OPEN } from "../lib/hosted";

const UPDATED = "27 October 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Surfing Dog"
  description="What Surfing Dog knows about you, which is almost nothing if you run the inbox yourself, and what will change when we host it for you."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="privacy-h">What we know about you</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Surfing Dog Inbox runs on your own server or cloud account, so we know almost nothing about you. Search needs no
      sign-in and does not keep your searches; the one exception, an error log, is explained below. This page lists
      exactly what we do see.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: privacy@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="self-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="self-h">If you run the inbox yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Your customers' messages, bookings and orders stay in your database. We never receive them, so we can't read
        or recover them. The code is open if you want to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some things leave your inbox, and only for a <b>network</b> it reports to, in Settings → Networks. The setup
        offers network.surfingdog.ai, switched on; you can switch it, and any other, off again. A network your inbox
        reports to gets:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Your inbox's address</b>, once, so it can check your inbox's public profile, and <b>every hour</b> your
          software version, what it runs on, and how many bookings, orders, quotes and messages arrived in the last 24
          hours. Counts only. With your secret key set, this hourly message is signed, and the network answers it with
          your own standing there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Every receipt your inbox signs</b>: when a booking is confirmed or an order accepted or paid, and again
          when it ends (it happened, the customer did not turn up, either side cancelled, it was delivered). A receipt
          says which business issued it, your reference for the booking or order, what kind of promise it is, when it
          was due and when it was made, the amount if there is a price, and how an order was paid when your inbox
          knows. The customer appears as a code, never a name: an HMAC made with a key only your inbox holds, which
          the network can't turn back into a person, and the same customer at another business gets a different
          code. When the customer's assistant presented their pass, the receipt also names that presentation, one
          per network, so the network that issued the pass counts the outcome on that person's record; every network
          the receipt goes to sees these references.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A counter-signature</b>, if the customer's assistant signs a receipt: its signature and its public key.
          An assistant that keeps its key uses the same one at every business, so the receipts it signed can be
          linked to each other. It may also name the customer's pass, by a reference that is not the pass itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A new customer's email address</b>, when a customer books or orders with an email and their assistant
          carries no pass. With it go your reference for that booking or order and the name of their assistant
          (and a fingerprint of its public key, and its platform, when it signs). The network uses the address to give the customer a
          <b>key</b>, a secret that lets any business on that network recognise them later. Your inbox asks the network
          to email the key to the customer itself, as one line in your name: a code for their assistant, with a link to
          the network's page where they can see and correct their record, and a "Not you?" link. The code and the
          links carry the network's address; the email uses no other words of the network's. The network keeps the
          address only until then. A network that leaves that email to your inbox has your inbox send it; turn that
          line off under Customers you already know. Each network has its own switch for this, "Give first-time
          customers a key": off when your inbox joins network.surfingdog.ai at setup or on our hosting.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A pass or a key the customer's assistant carries</b>, only to the network that issued it, with the
          customer's email address when their request has one, so the network can say how that customer keeps their
          bookings and whether the address is theirs. An assistant that signs its requests carries a reference
          instead of the pass, and your inbox passes on its signature: the address, path and query of the request, a
          hash of what it sent, its content type, its public key and the pass references it signed. Your inbox never
          passes on a signature that holds a secret, or that covers any other header.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Never sent: message text, names, phone numbers, postal addresses, what was ordered, payment references.
      </p>
      <p>
        What your inbox keeps of this, in your database: what each network said about a customer (a code for that
        customer at your business, and their standing) on the booking or order and on the customer; a hash of each
        pass it saw, to recognise it again; a network's answers for an hour, a day for "already known", filed under
        hashes. A key a customer's assistant presents is passed on and never stored. A new customer's key is kept
        sealed with your secret key until it is emailed to them, and their first pass sealed so the assistant that
        made the booking or order can collect it again; neither longer than seven days, and neither shown to you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn a network off and nothing more is sent to it. What it already holds, it keeps under its own rules. Anyone
        can run a network, and one we don't run is not covered by this page: add only networks you trust.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">If we host it for you</h2>
      {
        HOSTED_PILOT_OPEN ? (
          <p>
            You are the controller of the data your customers send your hosted inbox, and we are your processor, under
            the <a class="link" href="/hosted/processing/">processing agreement</a>. For your own account we are the
            controller: the <a class="link" href="/hosted/privacy/">hosted privacy notice</a> says what we keep and why.
            Your hosted inbox joins network.surfingdog.ai the day it goes live and sends it what the list above says;
            switch it off in Settings → Networks.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <>
            <p>
              Hosting is not open yet. When it opens, you will be the controller of the data your customers send you, and
              we will be your processor. We will hold it on our own servers in the European Union, store its encrypted
              backups in the European Union too, and use it only to run the service for you. For your own account, your
              address, your billing and how you use the service, we will be the controller.
            </p>
            <p>
              You will be able to export everything as a file, and delete everything, from the software without asking
              us. A written processing agreement will be part of the terms before the first customer is onboarded.
            </p>
          </>
        )
      }

      <h2 class="h-section">This website</h2>
      <p>
        No advertising or tracking pixels, and no cookie banner, because there is nothing to consent to. We count visits
        to this website with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookies and does not follow you across sites: it
        records the page, the site you came from, your country and your browser, and is never on a business's inbox.
        Our servers keep technical logs to keep the site up and stop abuse; what they hold, and when an IP address is
        among it, is under <a class="link" href="#search-h">Search</a> below. Cloudflare sits in front of the site and
        keeps its own logs for the same reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you write to us, we keep your message and email address so we can answer, and use them
        for nothing else. Posts on the requests board are public. The email address you give there is used only to
        tell you when your request ships.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section" id="search-h">Search, its MCP server and the checker</h2>
      <p>
        Search answers at <code>surfingdog.ai/search</code>, over MCP at <code>surfingdog.ai/mcp</code>, over A2A
        and through the REST API. It needs no sign-in, sets no cookie, and gives everyone the same answer.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Searches.</b> The words, filters and place of a search are used to answer and then dropped. The service's
          log leaves them out (over the REST API they travel in the query string, which it does not record), and
          nothing ties them to you. The one exception is an error at our front server, under Logs and limits below.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Checks.</b> When you, or an AI for you, ask to check a website (at <code>/check</code> or with
          <code>check_business</code>), we keep the website's domain, when and how the check was asked for (the page,
          MCP or A2A), how many times, and what reading the site found. We do not keep who asked. The record of each
          new read of a site is deleted after 90 days. The result is public at <code>surfingdog.ai/b/&lt;domain&gt;</code>;
          a business can hide its own with <code>update_business</code>, with proof that the domain is its own, and can
          ask us to stop listing or reading its site at <a class="link" href="/bot">surfingdog.ai/bot</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Doors.</b> We read the doors businesses publish for AI agents (their MCP servers, agent cards, inboxes and
          the like). Each read itself is kept 90 days. What they found we keep for good, one line per door per day: how
          many reads we made, how many were answered, failed (and why) or blocked, how quickly, the protocol version the
          door states, and the first and last day it answered. Beyond the site's own web addresses it holds nothing
          about a person, no page content and nothing a customer wrote. {doorHistoryPublic(DOOR_SHARE_SHOWN)} A business
          that opts out at <a class="link" href="/bot">surfingdog.ai/bot</a> has all of it deleted at once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Registering and changing a listing.</b> <code>register_business</code> and <code>update_business</code>
          keep what a business's AI sends: the listing's facts (name, category, description, address or service area,
          hours, languages, doors), which are public in the directory; the version of the listing terms it agreed to,
          and when; the kind of proof it used; and a record of each change (which fields, with which proof, when),
          which has no time limit today. A phone number or an email address in its text is dropped, never kept.
          Proof is kept as keyed hashes, never as the secret itself: a challenge until a day after it expires, with a
          keyed hash of the network address that asked for it, to count a day's challenges; a code sent to an address
          at the domain until a day after it expires, and that address's hash for 30 days after its code is used; a
          claim token for its 90 days and 30 days after. For a listing claimed with a code, the address's hash stays
          with the listing, so a code sent to another address cannot take it over.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Requests we carry to a business.</b> {CARRY_PROMISE} {HOSTED_KEEPS} When your assistant asks us to send a
          business your question, a request for a quote or a request for a booking, we pass your words, the details of
          the request (a service, a time, a quantity, the language you want), your email address and the name you give,
          if any, to that business's own inbox, and nothing else; we write none of it down. With it go a key made from
          the request itself, so that a retry is not sent twice, and a code that tells the business's inbox which
          requests came from the same sender that day, without saying who; it is different for each business and each
          day. We keep a record that it was delivered: when, to which business, which kind of request and how it went,
          never who sent it or what it said, for 400 days. The business alone is told how many arrived. To apply the
          limits we count, in memory, how often one person, one address, one email address (as a keyed hash) and the
          same words (as a keyed hash, for about an hour) are sent, each forgotten a day after its last use at most.
          We never carry a phone number. Your request is then the business's, in its inbox, as any customer's is, and
          it can answer you by email.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Logs and limits.</b> The service's own log records each request's method, path and how long it took: not
          your IP address, not a search's words, and not a tool's arguments. A path can name a business, such as the
          domain of one listing or check result looked up, but not who looked. How often one address, or one session
          of an assistant, calls is counted in memory, to apply the published limits, and never written down, except
          the keyed hash a register challenge keeps (above). An error at our front server (for example while the service restarts) can be logged with the
          request's IP address, the address it asked for (a REST search's words included) and its headers. These logs
          are kept in the server's system log, which is trimmed by size, with no fixed time limit yet.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="h-section" id="phone-h">The phone app</h2>
      <p>
        Surfing Dog Inbox for Android talks to the inboxes you connect it to, and to Google for notifications. It
        sends us nothing about how you use it, and no crash reports.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Your inbox</b> gets the phone's maker and model as its name, the phone's public keys and its push token.
          You see each phone, and remove it, in Settings → Phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>The camera</b> reads the code from your inbox inside the app. The picture never leaves the phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Notifications</b> are encrypted by your inbox for your phone alone, then sent through our relay at
          <code>push.surfingdog.ai</code> to Google's Firebase Cloud Messaging. The relay keeps, for each phone, the
          inbox it belongs to, its push token (encrypted) and when it last delivered. It cannot read what it carries,
          keeps no message, and deletes a phone nothing reached for 90 days. Google sees the push token, a Firebase
          installation ID, when each message arrives and how big it is, under Google's own privacy policy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Updates.</b> At most twice a day the app reads <code>surfingdog.ai/phone/latest.json</code> to see
          whether a new version is out: an ordinary request, logged as any visit to this website is. The app itself
          comes from GitHub, under GitHub's privacy statement.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>On the phone</b>, what the app keeps is encrypted with a key in the phone's Android Keystore, and is never
          backed up. Remove an inbox from the phone in the app's Settings, or uninstall the app, and it is
          gone.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="h-section">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to correct or delete it, object to what
        we do with it, and complain to a supervisory authority. For the network, deleting means we remove what links a
        record to you (your email's code, your keys and passes); the coded records of past bookings stay, because
        businesses' records are made of them. In Portugal that is the
        <a class="link" href="https://www.cnpd.pt/" rel="noopener">CNPD</a>. Write to privacy@surfingdog.ai and we will
        answer within a month.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where we rely on legitimate interest rather than your consent, it is to keep the service running and prevent
        abuse, and you can object to that too.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section" id="network-h">Our network, and the customers of businesses on it</h2>
      <p>
        We run the network at <code>network.surfingdog.ai</code>. Businesses whose inbox reports to it are listed in
        its directory, and it keeps a record of how reliably businesses and their customers keep bookings and orders.
        It uses only real transactions, and it never refuses or blocks anyone: it orders its directory by rules it
        publishes, and each business's own rules decide what a customer's record earns them, rules that may only
        speed things up or bring in a person, never refuse.
      </p>
      <p><b>What it keeps, and for how long.</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The hourly messages, with the internet address they came from, for 90 days. A business's listing (its public
          profile, version, whether it is answering, its counts and its standing), and snapshots of it, for good.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every receipt, counter-signature, and record of which business was shown which customer's standing, for
          good. A record that could quietly disappear would be worth nothing, so none does.
        </li>
        <li>
          For a customer: a record with no name, keyed by an HMAC of their email address, and an HMAC of its domain
          (never the address itself); the hashes of their key and passes (never the secrets, except a new customer's
          key and pass, kept encrypted for seven days so a retry never loses them, and a pass made from a key, kept
          encrypted for a day); the name, public key and platform of the assistants that carried
          them; and the outcomes of their bookings and orders, from receipts that name them by a code.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sign-in sessions for 24 hours, and emailed codes for ten minutes. A code is sent to the address as typed,
          and the address is not stored.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>Who sees what.</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A business's standing (its score and tier, and how many promises it kept and broke) is public, in the
          directory. Nothing about any customer is ever in it.
        </li>
        <li>
          A customer's standing is seen by a business only when the customer's assistant presents their pass there.
          There is no way to look a person up. The business sees their tier, their score, how many promises they kept
          and broke and at how many businesses, since when the network has known them, whether they proved their
          email address, and whether their pass was used unusually. It never sees their email address or which other
          businesses they used, and each business gets a different code for the same person.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>If you are a customer.</b></p>
      <p>
        You never sign up. If you book or order with an email at a business on the network, you may get an email with
        your key. You can give it to any assistant, or ignore it. With a code sent to your address you can sign in at
        the network to see your record, every business that was shown it, and the passes your assistants hold; revoke
        a pass; replace a lost key (your record carries over); and contest an outcome you think is wrong. Or write to
        us.
      </p>
      <p>
        The network's rules, in plain words and as numbers, are on <a class="link" href="/network/">the network page</a>
        and at <code>network.surfingdog.ai/v1/ranking</code>, with every change announced fifteen days before it
        applies.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        The date at the top shows the last change. The site is an open repository, so every edit to this page is a
        public commit. We will announce material changes on the blog.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">Where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          We wrote this from what the software does today and checked it against the code. A lawyer has not reviewed
          it yet. That happens before hosting opens to anyone but us, and this note comes off the page once it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
